WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.620
Cockroaches are nature’s most successful

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:04.720
crash test dummies.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.480
Roaches don’t swerve or slow down

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:07.759
when they encounter walls.

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:09.660
And it's not because they can’t see them.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.000
It’s because walls are no obstacle.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.560
These unstoppable insects

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.360
hit a wall and keep on running--up.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.120
They use the force of the collision

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:20.980
to launch themselves vertically,

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:24.250
a process that takes just 75 msec.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:26.900
Now, researchers are taking a page

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:28.500
from the cockroach playbook

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.740
to create their own wall-climbing bot.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.460
Most mobile robots deal with obstacles

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.900
by sensing and then dodging around them.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.900
But this decision-making process

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.020
slows them down and limits their ability

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.440
to perform high-speed tasks.

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:45.600
So scientists developed a sensorless,

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.500
cockroach-sized robot

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.140
with a soft exoskeleton and six legs

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:53.040
It, too, can shift from

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.200
horizontal to vertical movement

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.460
by simply crashing into a wall.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:59.140
The big secret to its success

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:02.180
is its tiny size—a mere 16 grams.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.240
As animals increase in size,

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:07.180
they hit with more energy during collisions,

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:09.450
making them more susceptible to injury.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.160
The researchers calculated

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.210
that only animals weighing less than 1 kg

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:15.940
would be able to use the cockroaches’

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.970
head-first collision technique for climbing.

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:21.220
Sad news for real crash test dummies,

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.020
who had better keep buckling up.

